0.0087  Tc 2.1813  Tw (Difference Engines) Tj79.08 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0075  Tc 2.1825  Tw ( [Swade93]. Although) Tj-267.12 -12  TD 0.0128  Tc 0.9572  Tw (these engines use the decimal number system rather than the binary representation now) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0154  Tc 0.4509  Tw (common in modern electronics, the underlying concepts are very similar. The ) Tj318.36 0  TD 0.0147  Tc 0  Tw (Analytical) Tj-318.36 -12  TD 0.0141  Tc 0.1686  Tw (Engine, developed in 1834, was perceived as a general-purpose computing machine, with) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0112  Tc 0.8915  Tw (features strikingly close to modern computers. Besides executing the basic repertoire of) TjT* 0.0124  Tc 0.4976  Tw (operations \(addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division\) in arbitrary sequences, the) TjT* 0.0111  Tc 0.3589  Tw (machine operated in a two-cycle sequence, called \223store\224 and \223mill\224 \(execute\), similar to) TjT* 0.0154  Tc -0.1311  Tw (current computers. It even used pipelining to speed up the execution of the addition opera-) TjT* 0.0105  Tc -0.1282  Tw (tion! Unfortunately, the complexity and the cost of the designs made the concept impracti-) TjT* 0.013  Tc 0.0995  Tw (cal. For instance, the design of Difference Engine I \(part of which is shown in Figure 1.1\)) TjT* 0.0113  Tc 0.0187  Tw (required 25,000 mechanical parts at a total cost of \24317,470 \(in 1834!\).) TjET1 1 1 rg 144 113.04 202.08 218.52 re fq 202.08 0 0 -218.52 144 331.56 cm /im1 Doendstreamendobj224 0 obj3485 endobj225 0 obj<< /Type /XObject /Subtype /Image /Name /im1 /Width 269 /Height 291 /BitsPerComponent 8 /ColorSpace [ /Indexed /DeviceRGB 255 227 0 R ] /Length 226 0 R >> stream
