 Tw (Photoresist development and bake) Tj138.96 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0144  Tc 0.4956  Tw ( \227 the wafers are developed in either an acid or) Tj-138.96 -12  TD 0.0175  Tc 1.4525  Tw (base solution to remove the non-exposed areas of photoresist. Once the exposed) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0134  Tc 0.0997  Tw (photoresist is removed, the wafer is \223soft-baked\224 at a low temperature to harden the) TjT* 0.0194  Tc -0.1094  Tw (remaining photoresist.) Tj-11.04 -18  TD /F2 9.96  Tf0.045  Tc 0  Tw (5.) Tj11.04 0  TD /F3 9.96  Tf0.0071  Tc 0.0229  Tw (Acid Etching ) Tj54.6 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0187  Tc 0.0713  Tw (\227 material is selectively removed from areas of the wafer that are not) Tj-54.6 -12  TD 0.0149  Tc 1.7824  Tw (covered by photoresist. This is accomplished through the use of many different) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0192  Tc 0.7908  Tw (types of acid, base and caustic solutions as a function of the material that is to be) TjT* 0.0183  Tc 0.2886  Tw (removed. Much of the work with chemicals takes place at large wet benches where) TjT* 0.0124  Tc 0.1276  Tw (special solutions are prepared for specific tasks. Because of the dangerous nature of) TjT* 0.0152  Tc -0.0052  Tw (some of these solvents, safety and environmental impact is a primary concern. ) Tj-11.04 -18  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.045  Tc 0  Tw (6.) Tj11.04 0  TD /F3 9.96  Tf0.0126  Tc 0.4574  Tw (Spin, rinse, and dry) Tj79.8 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0154  Tc 0.3964  Tw ( \227 a special tool \(called SRD\) cleans the wafer with deionized) Tj-79.8 -12  TD 0.011  Tc 1.0335  Tw (water and dries it with nitrogen. The microscopic scale of modern semiconductor) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0155  Tc -0.1826  Tw (devices means that even the smallest particle of dust or dirt can destroy the circuitry.) TjT* 0.0139  Tc 0.9215  Tw (To prevent this from happening, the processing steps are performed in ultra-clean) TjT* 0.0094  Tc -0.0274  Tw (rooms where the number of dust particles per cubic foot of air ranges between 1 and) TjT* 0.0218  Tc 2.9122  Tw (10. Automatic wafer handling and robotics are used whenever possible. This) TjT* 0.0101  Tc 1.1899  Tw (explains why the cost of a state-of-the-art fabrication facility easily ranges in the) TjT* 0.013  Tc 1.437  Tw (multiple billions of dollars. Even then, the wafers must be constantly cleaned to) TjT* 0.0111  Tc 0.008  Tw (avoid contamination, and to remove the left-over of the previous process steps.) Tj-11.04 -18  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.045  Tc 0  Tw (7.) Tj11.04 0  TD /F3 9.96  Tf0.0226  Tc 0.2474  Tw (Various process steps) Tj87.72 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0161  Tc 0.2724  Tw ( \227 the exposed area can now be subjected to a wide range of) Tj-87.72 -12  TD 0.0149  Tc 0.4296  Tw (process steps, such as ion implantation, plasma etching, or metal deposition. These) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0113  Tc 0.0187  Tw (are the subjects of the subsequent section.) Tj-11.04 -18  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.045  Tc 0  Tw (8.) Tj11.04 0  TD /F3 9.96  Tf0.0147  Tc 0.0953  Tw (Photoresist removal \(or ashing\)) Tj128.28 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0121  Tc 0.0929  Tw ( \227 a high-temperature plasma is used to selectively) Tj-128.28 -12  TD 0.0152  Tc -0.0023  Tw (remove the remaining photoresist without damaging device layers.) Tj0 -18  TD 0.01  Tc 0.8  Tw (We illustrate the use of the photolitographic process for one specific example, the) Tj-24 -12  TD 0.0117  Tc 0.7863  Tw (patterning of a layer of SiO) Tj113.52 -2.4  TD /F1 6.96  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (2) Tj3.48 2.4  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.03  Tc (,) Tj2.52 -2.4  TD /F1 6.96  Tf0  Tc 0.06  Tw ( ) Tj2.28 2.4  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.0209  Tc 0.7411  Tw (in Figure 2.5. The sequence of process steps shown in the) Tj-121.8 -12  TD 0.0124  Tc 0.1192  Tw (Figure patterns exactly one layer of the semiconductor material, and may seem very com-) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0146  Tc -0.0971  Tw (plex. Yet, the reader has to bear in mind that same sequence patterns the layer of ) Tj323.16 0  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.0211  Tc -0.1111  Tw (the com-) Tj-323.16 -12  TD 0