w (area. This effect is called \223) Tj103.44 0  TD /F3 9.48  Tf-0.0046  Tc 0  Tw (self-loading) Tj45.36 0  TD /F2 9.48  Tf0.009  Tc 0.306  Tw (\224. In addition, wide transistors have a larger gate) Tj-148.8 -12  TD 0.0043  Tc 0.3057  Tw (capacitance, which increases the fan-out factor of the driving gate and adversely affects) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0095  Tc -0.0395  Tw (its speed. ) Tj-12 -18  TD 0.042  Tc 0  Tw (\225) Tj12 0  TD /F3 9.48  Tf0  Tc 1.23  Tw (Increase ) Tj1.2877  Tc 0  Tw (V) Tj42.12 -2.4  TD /F3 6.6  Tf-0.0852  Tc (DD) Tj9.6 2.4  TD /F2 9.48  Tf0.0059  Tc 1.2241  Tw (. As illustrated in Figure 5.17, the delay of a gate can be modulated by) Tj-51.72 -12  TD 0.0067  Tc -0.2367  Tw (modifying the supply voltage. This flexibility allows the designer to trade-off energy dis-) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0064  Tc 0.255  Tw (sipation for performance, as we will see in a later section. However, increasing the sup-) TjT* 0.0064  Tc 1.8036  Tw (ply voltage above a certain level yields only very minimal improvement and hence) TjT* 0.0059  Tc 1.5441  Tw (should be avoided. Also, reliability concerns \(oxide breakdown, hot-electron effects\)) TjT* 0.0107  Tc -0.0407  Tw (enforce firm upper-bounds on the supply voltage in deep sub-micron processes.) TjET1 1 1 rg 108 397.8 396 13.2 re f491.64 398.64 12 12 re f0 0 0 rg 491.64 410.4 12.24 0.48 re f503.4 398.4 0.48 12.24 re f491.4 398.4 12.24 0.48 re f491.4 398.64 0.48 12.24 re f0.502 0.502 0.502 rg 496.08 404.64 7.56 6 re f0 0 0 rg 496.08 410.4 7.8 0.48 re f503.4 404.4 0.48 6.24 re f495.84 404.4 7.8 0.48 re f495.84 404.64 0.48 6.24 re f1 1 1 rg 496.08 398.64 7.56 6 re f0 0 0 rg 496.08 404.4 7.8 0.48 re f503.4 398.4 0.48 6.24 re f495.84 398.4 7.8 0.48 re f495.84 398.64 0.48 6.24 re f1 1 1 rg 108 367.8 396 12 re f0 0 0 rg 143.88 372.48 0.24 0.24 re fq 144 372.36 360 0.36 re h W n 144 372.72 m 144 372.48 l 504 372.48 l 504 372.72 l h fQ BT144 361.8  TD/F0 9  Tf-0.0204  Tc 0.0504  T