ng the \(normalized\) VTC for different supply voltages not only confirms this) Tj-16.2 -12  TD 0.0123  Tc 0.3857  Tw (conjecture, but even shows that the inverter is well and alive for supply voltages close to) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0118  Tc -0.0458  Tw (the threshold voltage of the composing transistors \(Figure 5.12a\). At a voltage of 0.5 V \227) TjT* 0.0146  Tc 0.3829  Tw (which is just 100 mV above the threshold of the transistors \227 the width of the transition) TjT* 0.015  Tc -0.1875  Tw (region measures only 10% of the supply voltage \(for a maximum gain of 35\), while it wid-) TjT* 0.0241  Tc 0.9059  Tw (ens to 17% for 2.5 V. So, given this improvement in dc characteristics, why do we not) TjT* 0.0181  Tc 1.1288  Tw (choose to operate all our digital circuits at these low supply voltages? Three important) TjT* 0.0249  Tc -0.0349  Tw (arguments come to mind:) Tj12 -18  TD -0.006  Tc 0  Tw (\225) Tj12 0  TD 0.0169  Tc 0.1931  Tw (In the following sections, we will learn that reducing the supply voltage indiscrimi-) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0056  Tc 0.3644  Tw (nately has a positive impact on the energy dissipation, but is absolutely detrimental) TjT* 0.0191  Tc -0.0371  Tw (to the performance on the gate.) Tj-12 -18  TD -0.006  Tc 0  Tw (\225) Tj12 0  TD 0.0106  Tc 2.2327  Tw (The dc-characteristic becomes increasingly sensitive to variations in the device) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0132  Tc 0.3659  Tw (parameters such as the transistor threshold, once supply voltages and intrinsic volt-) TjT* 0.0196  Tc -0.0496  Tw (ages become comparable.) Tj-12 -18  TD -0.006  Tc 0  Tw (\225) Tj12 0  TD 0.0145  Tc 1.2046  Tw (Scaling the supply voltage means reducing the signal swing. While this typically) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0139  Tc 1.4132  Tw (helps to reduce the internal noise in the system \(such as caused by crosstalk\), it) TjT* 0.0158  Tc -0.0158  Tw (makes the design more sensitive to external noise sources that do not scale.) TjET1 1 1 rg 109.68 495.48 193.32 161.52 re f109.