roaches) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0129  Tc 0.7001  Tw (using the CMOS technology. Each of the circuit styles has its advantages and disadvan-) TjT* 0.0129  Tc 0.2201  Tw (tages. Which one to select depends upon the primary requirement: ease of design, robust-) TjT* 0.0163  Tc 0.0395  Tw (ness, area, speed, or power dissipation. No single style optimizes all these measures at the) TjT* 0.0139  Tc 1.5676  Tw (same time. Even more, the approach of choice may vary from logic function to logic) TjT* -0.0052  Tc 0  Tw (function.) Tj24 -12  TD 0.0187  Tc 0.0884  Tw (The static approach has the advantage of being robust in the presence of noise. This) Tj-24 -12  TD 0.0143  Tc -0.1505  Tw (makes the design process rather trouble-free and amenable to a high degree of automation.) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0129  Tc -0.1114  Tw (This ease-of-design does not come for free: for complex gates with a large fan-in, comple-) TjT* 0.0141  Tc 0.7359  Tw (mentary CMOS becomes expensive in terms of area and performance. Alternative static) TjT* 0.0103  Tc 0.2597  Tw (logic styles have therefore been devised. Pseudo-NMOS is simple and fast at the expense) TjT* 0.0147  Tc 0.6353  Tw (of a reduced noise margin and static power dissipation. Pass-transistor logic is attractive) TjT* 0.0112  Tc 0.8403  Tw (for the implementation of a number of specific circuits, such as multiplexers and XOR-) TjT* 0.0174  Tc -0.0114  Tw (dominated logic such as adders. ) Tj24 -12  TD 0.0171  Tc 1.1206  Tw (Dynamic logic, on the other hand, makes it possible to implement fast and small) Tj-24 -12  TD 0.0159  Tc 0.8798  Tw (complex gates. This comes at a price. Parasitic effects such as charge sharing make the) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0129  Tc 1.0325  Tw (design process a precarious job. Charge leakage forces a periodic refresh, which puts ) Tj0.9778  Tc 0  Tw (a) TjT* 0.0192  Tc -0.0342  Tw (lower bound on the operating frequency of the circuit.) Tj24 -12  TD 0.0168  Tc 0.2132  Tw (The current trend is towards an increased u