roduce the complementary signal. It has been) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0058  Tc 0.6242  Tw (observed that a differential implementation of a complex function may reduce the number of) TjT* 0.0038  Tc 1.4109  Tw (gates required by a factor of two! The number of gates in the critical) Tj0  Tc 0.03  Tw ( ) Tj281.64 0  TD -0.007  Tc 0  Tw (timing) Tj24.84 0  TD 0.0101  Tc 1.3399  Tw ( path is often) Tj-306.48 -12  TD 0.0067  Tc 0.7333  Tw (reduced as well. Finally, the approach prevents some of the time-differential problems intro-) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0066  Tc 0.3834  Tw (duced by additional inverters. For example, in logic design it often happens that both a signal) TjT* 0.0034  Tc 0.6157  Tw (and its complement are needed simultaneously. When the complementary signal is generated) TjT* 0.0027  Tc -0.1007  Tw (using an inverter, the inverted signal is delayed with respect to the original \(Figure 6.32a\). This) TjT* 0.0033  Tc 0.0267  Tw (causes timing problems, especially in very high-speed designs. The differential output capabil-) TjT* 0.0122  Tc -0.0422  Tw (ity avoids this problem \(Figure 6.32b\). ) Tj24 -12  TD 0.0067  Tc -0.2167  Tw (With all these positive properties, why not always use differential logic? Well, the differ-) Tj-24 -12  TD 0.0038  Tc 0.3112  Tw (ential nature virtually doubles the number of wires that has to be routed, leading very often to) Tj0 -12  TD 0.003  Tc 0.717  Tw (unwieldy designs \(on top of the additional implementation overhead in the individual gates\).) TjT* 0.0039  Tc 0.0061  Tw (Additionally, the dynamic power dissipation is high.) TjET1 1 1 rg 108 132.84 396 13.2 re f491.64 133.56 12 12 re f0 0 0 rg 491.64 145.32 12.24 0.48 re f503.4 133.32 0.48 12.24 re f491.4 133.32 12.24 0.48 re f491.4 133.56 0.48 12.24 re f0.502 0.502 0.502 rg 496.08 139.56 7.56 6 re f0 0 0 rg 496.08 145.32 7.8 0.48 re f503.4 139.32 0.48 6.24 re f495.84 139.32 7.8 0.48 re f495.84 139.56 0.48 6.24 re f1 1 1 rg 496.08 133.56 7.56 6 re f0 0 